"{\"url\":\"https://registry.isometric.com/project/prj_1JQ76PQZX1S04S93\",\"title\":\"Reforesting Degraded Appalachian Mine and Agricultural Lands — Isometric\",\"head\":[\"UNDER VALIDATION\",\"BIOSPHERE\",\"Reforesting Degraded Appalachian Mine and Agricultural Lands\",\"Living Carbon\",\"->\",\"PROJECT DETAILS\",\"Expected first certificates\",\"2026\",\"Project code\",\"XDVS\",\"Crediting period\",\"11 May 2023 to 31 Dec 2064\"],\"issued_header\":null,\"buffer_header\":null,\"crediting\":null,\"verifier\":null,\"code\":\"XDVS\",\"approval\":\"\",\"protocol_current\":\"Reforestation v1.1\",\"protocol_baseline\":null,\"cert_issued\":null,\"cert_retired\":null,\"cert_buffer\":null,\"reversal\":null,\"showing\":[\"Showing 1 of 1\"],\"tables\":[{\"heads\":[\"Submission date\",\"Title\",\"Download\"],\"rows\":[[\"08 Sep 2026\",\"PDD\",\"PDF | ->\"]]}],\"links\":[{\"h\":\"https://livingcarbon.com\",\"t\":\"Living Carbon\"},{\"h\":\"/protocol/reforestation/1.1\",\"t\":\"Reforestation v1.1\"},{\"h\":\"/supplier/spl_1JKG9W7P11S0BJZ8\",\"t\":\"Learn more\"},{\"h\":\"https://registry.isometric.com/standard#validation-and-verification-process\",\"t\":\"Isometric Standard\"},{\"h\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/isometric--production--eu--public-documents/spl_1JKG9W7P11S0BJZ8/prj_1JQ76PQZX1S04S93/pjd_1M213SV3D1S00RQT/pdd.pdf\",\"t\":\"PDF | ->\"}],\"text\":\"UNDER VALIDATION\\nBIOSPHERE\\nReforesting Degraded Appalachian Mine and Agricultural Lands\\nLiving Carbon\\n->\\nPROJECT DETAILS\\nExpected first certificates\\n2026\\nProject code\\nXDVS\\nCrediting period\\n11 May 2023 to 31 Dec 2064\\nPROTOCOL DETAILS\\nCurrent protocol version\\nReforestation v1.1\\nAccreditation\\nPublic comment period\\nThis project is in a public comment period from 08 Sep 2026 until 07 Oct 2026. Please send feedback to project-feedback@isometric.com.\\nSummary\\n\\nLiving Carbon restores degraded mining and agricultural lands in the Greater Appalachia Region through active reforestation and addressing financial and ecological barriers to natural forest regeneration. Former mine lands have severely compacted and nutrient-poor soils due to soil backfill compacted from decades of heavy machinery use that struggle to recover naturally, and are commonly dominated by invasive plants. Former agricultural lands also tend to exhibit extreme compaction and nutrient leaching following grazing or unsustainable practices that limit long-term economic viability and result in fallow lands commonly colonized with invasive plants that hinder natural forest succession. Our approach includes invasive plant removal, soil amendment, and planting diverse native trees species optimized for rapid forest cover, biodiversity, and carbon removal.\\n\\nAppalachia region\\nHow carbon is being removed\\n\\nBiosphere carbon dioxide removal (CDR) harnesses the natural capacity of terrestrial and near-coastal ecosystems to absorb and store carbon for extended periods. These pathways utilize the biological processes of forests, soils, wetlands, and other ecosystems to capture carbon dioxide from the atmosphere and sequester it in biomass, soil organic matter, and sediments. Approaches to biosphere CDR include Reforestation, Agroforestry, Improved Forest Management, Mangrove Restoration, Peatland Restoration, and Soil Carbon Enhancement. Biosphere pathways often provide many co-benefits around the protection and enhancement of biodiversity alongside sustainable development for local communities.\\n\\nLearn more\\nProject timeline\\nCompleted 2025\\nInitial documentation submission\\nCompleted 2025\\nAudit kick-off\\nCompleted 2026\\nSite visit\\nExpected 2026\\nIN PROGRESS\\nApproval date\\n\\nIsometric officially approves the project having reviewed the validation report produced by the VVB, documenting the activities, results, and conclusion of the audit. A full list of content requirements for the report can be found in the Isometric Standard.\\n\\nExpected 2026\\nInitial credit issuance\\nProject documentation\\nSubmission date\\tTitle\\tDownload\\n08 Sep 2026\\tPDD\\tPDF\\n->\\nShowing 1 of 1\\nPrevious\\nNext\"}"